This is a Web target. Drive the intended vulnerability over the application's
real topology.

- Reach the service the way a contestant does: through the real front door — the
  reverse proxy or application entry port — traversing any intermediate services
  (proxy, worker, Bot/browser) rather than poking an internal component directly.
- Drive the intended chain end to end — authentication bypass, SQL/NoSQL/command
  injection, SSRF, insecure deserialization, SSTI, or a Bot/XSS-triggered action
  — using the vulnerable dependency versions and app logic the environment
  preserves. Do not substitute a different, easier bug.
- Establish the session/auth state the chain requires (login, token, CSRF,
  cookie) and respect startup ordering: migrations, DB seed, and the Bot must be
  live before the step that depends on them.
- Capture the flag from wherever the app actually stores it — a database row, an
  admin-only route, a file the exploited process can read, or a Bot-only page —
  reached through the vulnerability, not through a maintenance or admin channel.
- The provisioned flag is injected fresh each run. Output the value your exploit
  pulls from the live app, never one copied from the writeup or seed data.
